# achievability_rubric.yaml
# Machine-readable companion to reference/ACHIEVABILITY-RUBRIC.md
#
# The scoring instrument. Consumed by tools/score-strategy.py.
#
# Two axes, never collapsed:
#   ACHIEVABILITY  -- can THIS team build it (13 factors, 0-5 each, weighted)
#   VALUE          -- is it worth building (5 factors, 0-5 each, weighted)
# The QUADRANT is the output. The combined index only orders rows inside it.
#
# Written 2026-08-22 for BIOCORE presented by Haas (FRC 2027, FIRST CANOPY),
# kickoff 2027-01-09 12:00 ET. NO GAME-SPECIFIC BIOCORE CLAIM APPEARS IN THIS FILE.
# BIOCORE's rules and scoring element are not public as of 2026-08-22; see
# research/00_PREMISE_CORRECTION.md.
#
# evidence: C = confirmed in a primary source in this corpus
#           H = historical pattern across prior FRC seasons, not stated for BIOCORE
#           S = speculation / model calibration
#           U = unverified

meta:
  version: 1
  written: 2026-08-22
  companion_md: reference/ACHIEVABILITY-RUBRIC.md
  scorer: tools/score-strategy.py
  value_axis_upstream: tools/cycle-model.py     # points/match is IMPORTED, not reimplemented
  inputs:
    capacity: reference/02_TEAM_CAPACITY_MODEL.md      # sec 8 constants
    archetypes: reference/archetype_corpus.yaml        # calibration + back-test set
    predictive: reference/04_predictive_factors.yaml   # every weight traces here
  team_profile: "~15 students, 1 experienced technical mentor, COTS path, $2,500 robot discretionary"

# =========================================================== team constants
# Copied verbatim from reference/02_TEAM_CAPACITY_MODEL.md sec 8. DO NOT EDIT HERE.
# Edit tools/capacity_model.py and re-derive. (Note: reference/team_capacity.yaml
# is referenced by that document but is NOT present on disk as of 2026-08-22 --
# these values are transcribed from its sec 8 table instead. See limitations.)
team_constants:
  evidence: S                      # model, calibrated; calendar inputs are C
  effective_build_hours: 599       # kickoff -> Week 1 event
  effective_build_hours_week4: 854
  effective_hours_multiplier: 0.352
  parallel_workstreams_max: 3      # incl. drivetrain and software
  novel_mechanisms_max: 2          # beyond the drivetrain
  novel_mechanisms_recommended: 1
  mechanism_hours_pool: 205        # CAD 75 + fabrication 130, the only pool a
                                   # new mechanism can be paid out of
  hours_per_novel_mechanism: 90    # capacity model route B, deliberately generous
  programming_hours: 135
  drive_practice_hours: 55         # ~9 h of real driver seat time before event 1
  real_driver_seat_hours: 9
  mentor_unblock_minutes_per_week: 204        # 3.4 h/wk
  budget_usd_robot_discretionary: 2500
  slack_hours: 0                   # capacity model sec 3.3 -- the plan closes at zero
  owned_manufacturing:             # what the shop actually has
    - hand
    - bandsaw+drill
    - 3dprint
  outsourced_2d_account: false     # set true once a SendCutSend-class account exists
                                   # and one test order has shipped (capacity sec 7.5)
  in_house_cnc: false

# ============================================================ ACHIEVABILITY
# 13 factors. 5 = EASIEST for this team. 0 = hardest / impossible.
# Every weight's `from` field names the number in 04_predictive_factors.yaml or
# 02_TEAM_CAPACITY_MODEL.md it is derived from. Weights are raw 0-100 and are
# normalised by their own sum at scoring time (sum = 856).
achievability:
  scale: {min: 0, max: 5, direction: "higher = more achievable"}
  weight_sum: 856
  factors:

  - id: A1
    name: build_hours_fit
    weight: 85
    from: "PF scope_discipline = 85. Hours are the currency scope is spent in; capacity constraint #3 (599 h, 205 h of it available to mechanisms)."
    measures: "Total CAD + fabrication + integration hours the design needs, against the 205 h mechanism pool."
    anchors:
      5: "<=60 h. A published/COTS design bolted on. Fabrication hours only, no design hours. Fits inside one mechanism slot with room for a second."
      3: "<=160 h. One novel mechanism at the model's generous 90 h plus its share of integration. Fits, but consumes most of the 205 h pool."
      1: "<=300 h. Exceeds the pool by up to 50%. Only buildable by cannibalising drive practice or the awards line."
      0: ">300 h. More than the entire CAD+fabrication budget. Not a scope question, an arithmetic one."
    tie_break: "If two scorers disagree, both write an hour estimate and average them; the band, not the point estimate, is the output."

  - id: A2
    name: parallel_workstream_demand
    weight: 85
    from: "PF scope_discipline = 85. Capacity constraint #2 (5 unsupervised leads); parallel_workstreams_max = 3, quadruply derived (capacity sec 5.2 routes A/B/C + sec 6.3 mentor route D)."
    measures: "How many concurrent development threads the design adds ON TOP of the three mandatory ones (drivetrain, primary mechanism, software)."
    anchors:
      5: "0 new streams. Software-only, or a variation inside an existing stream (a different hood angle, a second auto path)."
      3: "1 new stream. This IS the primary scoring mechanism -- the second of the three mandatory streams. Normal, not free."
      1: "2 new streams. A scoring mechanism AND an endgame mechanism, or a mechanism plus a first-year swerve adoption (capacity sec 5.6)."
      0: ">=3 new streams, i.e. 4+ total. Over parallel_workstreams_max. GATE G2 fires."
    tie_break: "A stream needs all five of: its own design decision, its own prototype loop, its own fab-queue slot, its own unsupervised student lead, its own integration failure mode. 'Add a limelight' is not a stream. 'Add a second scoring mechanism' is."

  - id: A3
    name: mentor_supervision_load
    weight: 80
    from: "Capacity constraint #1 -- the top of the hierarchy, 204 unblock-minutes/week. No PF analogue exists (PF measures outcomes, not inputs), so it is set just under scope_discipline's 85."
    measures: "Mentor unblock minutes per week the design will consume, against the 204 available."
    anchors:
      5: "<=50 min/wk (<=25%). Pre-solved design, no machine supervision, students can proceed unsupervised for a week at a time."
      3: "<=150 min/wk (<=75%). One mechanism needing a weekly design review plus occasional saw supervision. The normal load for the primary mechanism."
      1: "<=300 min/wk (<=150%). Needs the mentor present for most of the work, or needs mill/lathe supervision every session. Starves the other two streams."
      0: ">300 min/wk. The mentor becomes the mechanism (capacity sec 6.5). GATE G5 fires."
    tie_break: "Count only unblock and supervision minutes, not teaching. Teaching is a separate 4.1 h/wk line."

  - id: A4
    name: drive_practice_sensitivity
    weight: 90
    from: "PF drive_practice = 90, verbatim -- the highest weight in the measured corpus. Capacity sec 3.4 funds only ~9 h of real driver seat time before a Week 1 event."
    measures: "How much of the design's value is unlocked only by driver hours this team does not have."
    anchors:
      5: "None. Value is banked by software or by a single scripted action. An AUTO routine or a stationary scorer."
      3: "Moderate. A driver learns the one repeated path in 2-3 hours; the rest is marginal gain."
      1: "High. Needs a driver who has run the cycle hundreds of times -- a full-field volume cycler, or a single-shot endgame under time pressure."
      0: "Extreme. The ENTIRE value is driver skill: defense, denial, contact play. Also fires the SOFT GATE downgrade at <20 available drive hours."
    tie_break: "Ask: if we gave this robot to a driver with 9 hours of seat time, what fraction of its ceiling do we get? >=80% -> 5; ~50% -> 3; ~25% -> 1; <15% -> 0."

  - id: A5
    name: reliability_exposure
    weight: 88
    from: "PF reliability = 88, verbatim [C]. One DQ halves pick probability (62-65% -> 22-42%) and moves median rank percentile from ~49 to 75-86."
    measures: "Serial-chain reliability. Every element that must work for the robot to score at all multiplies."
    anchors:
      5: ">=0.90 expected chain reliability. 0-1 elements in the critical chain (e.g. 0.93^1)."
      3: "0.80-0.85. Three elements in series, or two elements plus a sensing dependency."
      1: "0.65-0.72. Five elements in series. Fails in roughly one match in three."
      0: "<0.65. Six or more serial elements, or a known jam-prone path with no bypass."
    tie_break: "Use 0.93 per element for a small team's first-season mechanism; count sensing/vision as one extra element. State the element list, then compute -- do not eyeball."

  - id: A6
    name: graceful_degradation
    weight: 66
    from: "0.75 x PF reliability (88). It is the partial-credit half of the SAME evidence, so it is discounted rather than double-counted at full weight."
    measures: "When it breaks mid-match, what is left."
    anchors:
      5: "Rate drops, scoring continues. Or a second independent system keeps producing (plow keeps herding after the climber dies)."
      3: "The primary action is lost but the robot is still a legal, mobile, useful alliance partner (can play defense, can feed)."
      1: "The primary action is lost and the robot becomes a spectator, but is not a liability."
      0: "Total, and the failure state is a hazard or a foul source -- stuck extended, immobilised in a protected zone, entangling."
    tie_break: "Score the WORST single failure, not the average one."

  - id: A7
    name: programming_complexity
    weight: 70
    from: "No PF weight exists for programming. Derived from capacity sec 3.1-3.2: programming is the LARGEST single line in the 599 h budget (135 h, 22.5%) and runs at 79% programmer utilisation before a bug exists. Placed between scope_discipline (85) and cots_leverage (55)."
    measures: "Software difficulty AFTER the archetype_corpus systemcore_adjustment is applied."
    systemcore_note: "[C] 2027 replaces the roboRIO with SystemCore and WPILib 2027 is SystemCore-only. Add +0.0 to open-loop teleop, +0.5 to a single closed-loop mechanism, +1.0 to odometry/path-following, +1.0 to vision+pose fusion (and treat vision as UNAVAILABLE in build weeks 1-3)."
    anchors:
      5: "Adjusted difficulty <=1. Teleop button mapping. A first-year can write it."
      3: "Adjusted difficulty ~3. One closed-loop velocity or position controller with tuning, plus a one-element auto."
      1: "Adjusted difficulty ~5. Closed-loop control + odometry + a multi-step auto. Consumes both programmers for a month."
      0: "Adjusted difficulty >5. Vision-fused pose estimation driving a turret. In 2027 this is a research project, not a plan."
    tie_break: "Take programming_difficulty from archetype_corpus.yaml if the design matches an archetype; otherwise use its 1-5 scale definition, then add the SystemCore delta before banding."

  - id: A8
    name: sensing_vision_dependence
    weight: 30
    from: "PF vision_localization = 30, verbatim. Deliberately low on its own: it is a force multiplier on mechanisms that must already work."
    measures: "How much of the design stops working if the camera/coprocessor stack is not ready."
    anchors:
      5: "None or encoders only. Nothing to stop working."
      3: "Gyro + encoders. Field-oriented drive and dead-reckoning auto; degrades to open-loop."
      1: "Vision-assisted aiming or alignment. Works without it, but at a much lower rate."
      0: "Full pose fusion is load-bearing -- the design does not function without AprilTag localisation."
    tie_break: "Ask: if the coprocessor never boots at the event, does the robot still score? Yes, at full rate -> 5. Yes, slower -> 3-1. No -> 0."

  - id: A9
    name: manufacturing_floor
    weight: 45
    from: "PF custom_fabrication = 25 is the value-side weight. Promoted to 45 because capacity sec 5.2 route C (the single-machine shop queue) binds novel_mechanisms_max on its own and is the ONE route insensitive to headcount. 45 matches PF drivetrain_choice, likewise a 'matters through what it consumes' factor."
    measures: "The most capable machine any part of the design requires."
    anchors:
      5: "Hand tools, hardware-store stock, 3D print. No queue contention."
      3: "Bandsaw + drill press on stock tube/plate. Owned, but one of each -- queue contention with the drivetrain stream."
      1: "Router / waterjet / laser-cut sheet. NOT owned. Buildable only via an outsourced 2D account with a 1-2 week lead time. GATE G1 fires unless outsourced_2d_account is true."
      0: "In-house CNC mill or lathe for a load-bearing part. Not owned, not outsourceable at this budget. GATE G1 fires."
    tie_break: "Score the single hardest part, then check how MANY parts need that floor -- one router part is a purchase order, eight is a fabrication programme (this is the archetype_corpus fix for the G1/F4 mislabels)."

  - id: A10
    name: cots_availability
    weight: 55
    from: "PF cots_leverage = 55, verbatim [C]. 441 True Everybots in 2026, 100 alliance captains, 21 event wins, 22% captaincy against a 21.8% base rate."
    measures: "How much of the design can be bought, or copied from a released open design, instead of invented."
    anchors:
      5: "A released, documented, pre-solved design exists (Everybot/KitBot/vendor kit) and the team can copy it. Costs fabrication hours, near-zero design hours."
      3: "Core motion is COTS (gearbox, rollers, linear stage) but the geometry around it is bespoke."
      1: "Only fasteners and motors are COTS. Every functional surface is designed here."
      0: "Nothing exists to buy or copy; the concept itself has to be invented and validated."
    tie_break: "'A team posted a CAD of theirs on Chief Delphi' counts as 3, not 5 -- unreleased CAD without a build guide still costs design hours."

  - id: A11
    name: iteration_count
    weight: 60
    from: "0.7 x PF scope_discipline (85) = 60. Iteration is the mechanism BY WHICH scope discipline bites; capacity sec 3.3 shows SLACK = 0.0, so every unplanned prototype->revise loop is unfunded."
    measures: "Expected number of prototype -> test -> revise loops before the design is competition-ready."
    anchors:
      5: "<=1. Build it once from a known design; tune, do not redesign."
      3: "~3. Normal for a first-of-its-kind mechanism on a small team: a foamboard proof, a rough version, a final."
      1: "~5. Compliant-material handling, high-speed launching, or anything where the failure mode is only visible under match conditions."
      0: ">5, or the loop length is unknown because nobody on the team has built anything like it."
    tie_break: "Count loops that require re-fabricating a part, not software rebuilds."

  - id: A12
    name: marginal_cost
    weight: 40
    from: "Capacity sec 7.2: on the COTS path money binds LAST, #5 of 5, at $2,500 discretionary. Set BELOW cots_leverage (55) on purpose -- whether a part EXISTS to buy matters more than what it costs."
    measures: "Dollars beyond a base drivetrain + control system, using the HIGH end of the cost band."
    anchors:
      5: "<=$300. Hardware store, printed parts, spare motors already owned."
      3: "<=$1,200. One mechanism's worth of vendor parts. Roughly half the discretionary budget."
      1: "<=$2,500. Consumes the entire discretionary budget and leaves nothing for spares. GATE G3 is at the boundary."
      0: ">$2,500. Over budget. GATE G3 fires."
    tie_break: "Use the HIGH end of the band and add 40% -- archetype_corpus states its cost bands are +/-40%. Exclude drivetrain and control system; those are committed elsewhere."

  - id: A13
    name: schedule_critical_path_risk
    weight: 62
    from: "0.72 x PF scope_discipline (85) = 62. Capacity sec 3.3 (SLACK = 0.0 h) and sec 9.4 (losing ONE unsupervised lead halves novel_mechanisms_max) make serialisation the team's largest uninsured risk."
    measures: "How much of the work must happen in a fixed order, and how exposed that order is to one late delivery or one absent student."
    anchors:
      5: "Fully parallel or software-only. Can be finished in the last two weeks if everything else slips."
      3: "One serial dependency with a known lead time (a vendor part, a print). Slippable by a week without consequence."
      1: "A chain of 3+ serial dependencies, or it must be mounted before the electrical board can be finished."
      0: "Depends on something with an unknown ship date (2027: SystemCore hardware itself), or on one specific student being present."
    tie_break: "Draw the dependency chain. Count the longest path in weeks. <=1 week -> 5; ~3 weeks -> 3; ~5 weeks -> 1; unknown -> 0."

# =================================================================== VALUE
# 5 factors. 5 = most valuable. Separate axis; NEVER summed with achievability.
value:
  scale: {min: 0, max: 5, direction: "higher = more competitively valuable"}
  weight_sum: 290
  upstream: "V1 consumes tools/cycle-model.py model() output. Do not recompute points/match here."
  factors:

  - id: V1
    name: points_per_match_share
    weight: 80
    from: "PF scoring_output = 80, verbatim [C]. 'Avg Match' separates picked from unpicked better than qual rank, Ranking Score or Avg Auto in all four scraped seasons (AUC 0.80-0.87)."
    measures: "Expected points/match from tools/cycle-model.py, as a share of the season's MEDIAN alliance score. One robot of three is 0.33 = parity."
    anchors:
      5: ">=0.45 of median alliance score. Carries an alliance."
      3: "0.18-0.29. Below parity but a real contribution; the contested middle of the field."
      1: "0.05-0.09. Token scoring."
      0: "<0.05 direct AND no credited indirect effect."
    indirect_points_equivalent:
      evidence: S
      note: "Support and defense archetypes score 0 directly. Convert their effect to a point equivalent before banding. Defaults below are midpoints of the bands archetype_corpus.yaml itself states."
      defense_specialist: 45      # from 'denies 30-60 opponent points'
      feeder_support: 35          # from '+20-40% partner throughput'
    tie_break: "Run cycle-model.py at YOUR cycle time, not a good team's. Paste the TOTAL row."

  - id: V2
    name: rp_contribution
    weight: 55
    from: "Derived, not verbatim. PF measures Ranking Score as a WORSE predictor of being picked (AUC 0.777-0.821) than avg match points (0.801-0.872), so RP value is weighted BELOW scoring_output. Above defense because RP thresholds are the cheapest lever the corpus finds (archetype_corpus I4)."
    measures: "How much this design contributes toward a bonus/ranking point, especially one an alliance cannot reach without it."
    anchors:
      5: "Single-handedly clears, or nearly clears, an RP threshold that partners typically cannot (archetype_corpus I4: REBUILT TRAVERSAL 50 from one L3 + one L2)."
      3: "Contributes a meaningful, countable share of a threshold that the alliance reaches together."
      1: "Contributes incidentally; the RP would usually happen anyway."
      0: "No RP relevance."
    tie_break: "Read the RP table before scoring this factor. Buy the CHEAPEST tier that clears the threshold -- the threshold is almost never set at the top tier."

  - id: V3
    name: alliance_selection_appeal
    weight: 75
    from: "The terminal outcome the whole PF corpus predicts (pct_picked_no_DQ 61.7-64.7). Discounted BELOW scoring_output (80) because AUC 0.80-0.87 means avg-match points already explain most of it -- scoring both at full weight would double-count."
    measures: "Would a captain, watching one qualification match and reading the TBA page, want this robot."
    anchors:
      5: "Captain-tier / first-pick. The robot other teams build their alliance around."
      3: "Second pick. A specialist a captain deliberately shops for -- the endgame, the feeder, the defender."
      1: "Third pick / backup. Taken to fill a slot."
      0: "Unpicked. Nothing legible on the TBA page a captain values."
    tie_break: "The corpus outcome field is the anchor: event-winning/captain-tier -> 5, first-pick -> 4, second-pick -> 3, third-pick -> 2, unpicked -> 0."
    base_rate_note: "[C] 64-78% of event-WINNING alliances carried a below-median-OPR robot (78% in 2026). The second-pick slot is the statistically normal path, not a consolation prize."

  - id: V4
    name: defense_resistance
    weight: 35
    from: "PF defense_capability = 35, verbatim, used as the price of the defense INTERACTION. Game-conditional deltas in 04_predictive_factors.yaml (protected zones -8/zone, one-defender -15, pin count >=5 +10, foul cost +/-) apply to this weight on kickoff day."
    measures: "How much of this design's output survives a competent defender assigned to it."
    anchors:
      5: "Immune. Scores during AUTO (no defense by rule), or in the last seconds when defenders are elsewhere, or IS the defense."
      3: "Degraded but functional. Multiple approach angles, or a cycle short enough to complete between contacts."
      1: "Heavily suppressed. One approach path, or a long stationary dwell to score."
      0: "Neutralised. Requires a single surveyed position a defender can simply park on."
    tie_break: "Count how many distinct field positions the robot can score from. >=4 -> 5; 2-3 -> 3; 1 -> 1-0."

  - id: V5
    name: ceiling
    weight: 45
    from: "Set at PF drivetrain_choice's 45. Rationale: ceiling is what a BETTER version of this team would get, and PF's evidence (P90/P50 EPA ratio 1.38 -> 2.35) shows headroom accrues to teams this one is not. Weighted as a tiebreaker, not a driver."
    measures: "If everything goes right and the team gets 3 more weeks, how much better does this get."
    anchors:
      5: "Event-winning ceiling. More practice and more tuning keep paying, linearly, all season."
      3: "First/second-pick ceiling. Gets better for ~3 weeks then plateaus."
      1: "Hard-capped by the concept itself. A LEVEL-1 climb is 10 points forever."
      0: "No ceiling above its floor; the first working version is the last version."
    tie_break: "Ask what the SAME design looks like at a Week 6 event. If the answer is 'identical', score 1."

# ================================================================== GATES
# Hard disqualifiers, evaluated BEFORE scoring and independent of it.
# A gated design is capped at T4 GATED no matter how well it scores.
gates:
  policy: "A gate is not a penalty term. It is a statement that the design cannot be built by this team as specified. The correct response is ALWAYS to descope to a named variant and re-score, never to argue with the gate."
  checks:

  - id: G1
    name: MACHINE
    fires_when: "the design's manufacturing floor is not in team_constants.owned_manufacturing"
    exception: "floor == 'router' passes if outsourced_2d_account == true AND the part list is <=2 parts AND there are >=2 weeks of lead time left"
    hard: true
    from: "Capacity sec 5.2 route C / sec 7.1 constraint #4."
    descope_prompt: "Can the same function be made from tube + plate on the bandsaw, or 3D printed? If yes, that is a different candidate -- score it."

  - id: G2
    name: WORKSTREAMS
    fires_when: "new_workstreams + 3 mandatory > parallel_workstreams_max (3), i.e. any new stream beyond the primary mechanism; OR novel_mechanisms > novel_mechanisms_max (2)"
    hard: true
    from: "Capacity sec 5.2, quadruply derived (routes A/B/C + mentor route D at sec 6.3)."
    note: "2027 deductions apply BEFORE this check: vision-based pose estimation OR a first-year swerve adoption each reduce novel_mechanisms_max from 2 to 1 (capacity sec 5.6). Both together reduce it to 0."
    descope_prompt: "Which of the two mechanisms produces more points per hour? Build that one, and buy or copy the other."

  - id: G3
    name: BUDGET
    fires_when: "marginal_cost_high_usd > budget_usd_robot_discretionary (2500)"
    hard: true
    from: "Capacity sec 8.1. Uses the HIGH end of the band; archetype_corpus states bands are +/-40%."
    note: "Registration ($6,500) and travel do NOT trade against design decisions and are excluded by construction."
    descope_prompt: "Which single line item is >30% of the total? Is there a COTS substitute or a used-market equivalent?"

  - id: G4
    name: HOURS
    fires_when: "estimated_mechanism_hours > mechanism_hours_pool (205)"
    hard: true
    from: "Capacity sec 3.1 (CAD 75 + fabrication 130) and sec 3.3 (SLACK = 0.0)."
    descope_prompt: "Moving from a Week 1 to a Week 4 event buys +255 effective hours for free (capacity sec 7.4). Have you actually chosen your event yet? Round 1 preferencing opens 2026-09-24."

  - id: G5
    name: MENTOR
    fires_when: "estimated mentor unblock demand > mentor_unblock_minutes_per_week (204)"
    hard: true
    from: "Capacity sec 6.2-6.3. THE #1 constraint in the hierarchy."
    descope_prompt: "Recruiting one more technical mentor doubles this to 408 min/wk and is worth more than five more students (capacity sec 6.4). Deadline 2026-11-17."

  - id: G6
    name: DRIVE_HOURS
    fires_when: "drive_practice_sensitivity anchor score == 0 (extreme) AND real_driver_seat_hours < 20"
    hard: false                    # SOFT: one-tier downgrade, not a disqualification
    from: "archetype_corpus.yaml verdict_predicate.overriding_term_required, and PF drive_practice = 90."
    effect: "downgrade one tier (T1->T2, T2->T3)"
    descope_prompt: "This design's entire cost is driver hours. Either commit to a Week 3-4 event and a fall driving programme, or pick a design whose value is banked in software."

# ============================================================== AGGREGATION
aggregation:
  achievability_index: "100 * sum(w_i * s_i) / (5 * sum(w_i))     # 0-100"
  value_index: "100 * sum(v_j * t_j) / (5 * sum(v_j))             # 0-100"
  combined_index: "0.55 * achievability_index + 0.45 * value_index"
  combined_weighting_rationale: >
    Achievability is weighted higher than value on purpose. The failure mode this
    corpus actually measures for a 15-student team is SCOPE, not insufficient
    ambition: archetype_corpus invariant I2 (do-everything robot) has a 0/5
    small-team success rate across five seasons, while I6 (feeder/support) and I7
    (minimal flawless) are 5/5. The 55/45 split encodes that asymmetry without
    letting achievability alone decide -- a perfectly achievable robot that scores
    nothing still lands in CHEAP INSURANCE, not in BUILD THIS.
  no_zeros_in_green:
    rule: "ONE achievability factor scoring 0 caps the tier at T2 STRETCH; TWO OR MORE cap it at T3 RED."
    excludes: [A4]
    why: >
      A weighted mean can hide a single anchor written as impossible. A4 is excluded
      because it has its own dedicated gate (G6) and double-penalising it would drop
      every defense archetype archetype_corpus labels YES. This rule moved back-test
      agreement from 21/30 to 24/30, fixing exactly the AUTO-scope family (C5, P5, F5)
      that archetype_corpus predicted its own predicate would miss.
    order: "applied after the index bands, before the G6 soft downgrade."
  binding_constraint: >
    The first gate that fires, if any. Otherwise the achievability factor with the
    largest weighted deficit, w_i * (5 - s_i). This is the single thing to fix, and
    it is reported for every candidate whether it is gated or not.

# ================================================================ QUADRANTS
# The real output. Thresholds are the axis midpoints, nudged so that the corpus's
# hand-labelled YES archetypes land right of the achievability line.
quadrants:
  achievability_threshold: 60
  value_threshold: 55
  cells:
  - id: Q1
    name: BUILD THIS
    condition: "A >= 60 and V >= 55"
    decision_rule: >
      Commit on kickoff weekend. Assign an unsupervised lead by day 3 and CAD-freeze
      by day 10. If more than ONE candidate lands here, build the one with the lower
      A1 (build_hours_fit) deficit first and hold the other as the second mechanism
      only if novel_mechanisms_max still has room after the 2027 deductions.
  - id: Q2
    name: CHEAP INSURANCE
    condition: "A >= 60 and V < 55"
    decision_rule: >
      Do NOT lead with this, and do not skip it either. This is the second mechanism,
      the fallback, and the thing that keeps you a legal, useful alliance partner when
      the primary mechanism is in pieces. Build it AFTER the Q1 design passes its first
      reliability test, from spare hours, with a first-year pair leading it. It is also
      the Quality Award thesis (archetype_corpus I7 award_pairing).
  - id: Q3
    name: TRAP
    condition: "A < 60 and V >= 55"
    decision_rule: >
      The season-killer quadrant. Everything here is genuinely worth a lot of points
      and genuinely out of reach, which is exactly why teams pick it on kickoff day.
      The response is NEVER 'work harder'. It is: enumerate the descoped VARIANTS
      (fixed-position instead of turreted; one auto element instead of four; L1 climb
      instead of L3) and re-score each as its own candidate. archetype_corpus proves
      the variant, not the family, is what a small team should score -- invariant I1
      is 0/4 at full spec and 6/9 restricted to the fixed-range variant.
  - id: Q4
    name: DELETE
    condition: "A < 60 and V < 55"
    decision_rule: >
      Say it out loud in the strategy meeting and cross it off the whiteboard. Do not
      keep it as a 'maybe'. A live maybe consumes the scarcest resource in the model --
      mentor decision attention, 204 min/wk -- every week it stays on the board.

# ==================================================================== TIERS
tiers:
- id: T1
  name: GREEN
  range: "combined_index >= 62, no hard gate"
  plain_english: >
    Build it. This is inside your team's real capacity with enough margin that one bad
    week does not kill it. You will still have to protect the drive-practice and
    reliability hours -- being in GREEN means the design is not the thing that will
    beat you.
- id: T2
  name: STRETCH
  range: "48 <= combined_index < 62, no hard gate"
  plain_english: >
    Possible, and only possible, if you spend a specific named thing to buy it: a Week
    3-4 event instead of Week 1 (+255 effective hours, free), a second technical
    mentor, an outsourced-fabrication account, or a third programmer trained in the
    fall. Write down WHICH one before you start. A STRETCH design attempted without
    buying its enabler is how a season ends with an unfinished robot.
- id: T3
  name: RED
  range: "combined_index < 48, no hard gate"
  plain_english: >
    Do not build this. Not 'try and see' -- the combined index is low because several
    independent factors are all bad at once, and each one you fix reveals the next.
    If the VALUE axis is high, go read the TRAP quadrant rule and descope it into a
    different candidate.
- id: T4
  name: GATED
  range: "any hard gate fired, at any index"
  plain_english: >
    The design as specified cannot be built here. Not 'hard' -- it needs a machine you
    do not own, or more concurrent workstreams than you have leads for, or more money
    than exists. The index is printed for information only and MUST NOT be used to
    argue past the gate. Descope and re-score.

# =============================================== corpus -> factor auto-mapping
# Deterministic rules that let tools/score-strategy.py score archetype_corpus.yaml
# records directly, which is how the rubric is back-tested. Hand-authored candidate
# YAML should set factor scores EXPLICITLY; these mappings are a fallback so that a
# 30-record regression test exists at all. Every mapping is [S].
auto_score_from_corpus:
  evidence: S
  A1_hours_estimate: "90 * mechanism_count * mfg_multiplier + 15 * programming_difficulty; mfg_multiplier = {hand: 0.6, bandsaw+drill: 0.8, router: 1.1, CNC: 1.4}"
  A1_bands: [60, 110, 160, 205, 300]        # -> 5,4,3,2,1 ; else 0
  A2_from_mechanism_count: {0: 5, 1: 4, 2: 3, 3: 1, 4: 0}
  A3_minutes: "40 * mechanism_count + 30 if mfg_floor in {router, CNC}"
  A3_bands_ratio: [0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.5]
  A4_from_sensitivity: {low: 5, med: 3, high: 2, extreme: 0}
  A5_chain: "0.93 ** (mechanism_count + 1 if sensing in {vision-assist, full-pose} else mechanism_count)"
  A5_bands: [0.90, 0.85, 0.80, 0.72, 0.65]
  A6_from_reliability_profile: "GRACEFUL or PARTIAL -> 5 ; TOTAL -> 1 ; neither keyword -> 3"
  A7_systemcore_delta: {none: 0.0, encoders: 0.0, "gyro+encoders": 0.5, "vision-assist": 1.0, "full-pose": 1.0}
  A7_bands: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
  A8_from_sensing: {none: 5, encoders: 5, "gyro+encoders": 4, "vision-assist": 2, "full-pose": 1}
  A9_from_mfg_floor: {hand: 5, "bandsaw+drill": 4, router: 2, CNC: 0}
  A10_proxy: "{hand: 4, bandsaw+drill: 4, router: 3, CNC: 1} + 1 if mechanism_count <= 1, capped at 5"
  A10_caveat: "archetype_corpus has NO cots field. This is a proxy and the weakest mapping in the file."
  A11_iterations: "1 + 0.5*mechanism_count + (1 if programming_difficulty >= 4) + (1 if sensing in {vision-assist, full-pose})"
  A11_bands: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
  A12_bands_usd: [300, 700, 1200, 1800, 2500]
  A13_serial: "mechanism_count + (1 if mfg_floor in {router, CNC}) + (1 if sensing in {vision-assist, full-pose})"
  A13_bands: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
  V1_points: "points_per_cycle * est_cycles_per_match, or indirect_points_equivalent by invariant"
  V1_bands_share: [0.45, 0.30, 0.18, 0.10, 0.05]
  V2_by_invariant: {I1: 4, I2: 5, I3: 1, I4: 4, I5: 3, I6: 1, I7: 1}
  V3_from_outcome: {event-winning: 5, captain-tier: 5, first-pick: 4, second-pick: 3, third-pick: 2, unpicked: 0}
  V4_by_invariant: {I1: 2, I2: 2, I3: 5, I4: 4, I5: 5, I6: 4, I7: 4}
  V4_fixed_position_penalty: -1     # scoring_actions mentions ONE/fixed/surveyed position
  V5_from_outcome: {event-winning: 5, captain-tier: 4, first-pick: 4, second-pick: 2, third-pick: 1, unpicked: 0}

# ======================================================= kickoff-day hooks
# Filled on 2027-01-09 from the BIOCORE manual, before the rubric is run in anger.
biocore_kickoff_inputs:
  status: "ALL NULL by design. BIOCORE is not public as of 2026-08-22."
  median_alliance_score_estimate: null    # seed from a comparable season until real data exists
  scoring_table_rows: null                # >= 8 -> scope_discipline +10 (PF game_conditional)
  auto_gated_ranking_point: null          # true -> V2 weight +25 (PF game_conditional)
  protected_zone_rule_count: null         # V4 weight -8 per zone
  one_defender_at_a_time: null            # true -> V4 weight -15
  pin_count_seconds: null                 # >= 5 -> V4 weight +10
  foul_values_minor_major: null           # major >= 10% of typical score -> V4 -10, else +5
  systemcore_shipping: null               # false at kickoff -> A13 floor of 1 for anything needing it

limitations:
- "reference/team_capacity.yaml does not exist on disk as of 2026-08-22 although 02_TEAM_CAPACITY_MODEL.md sec 8 and 'Files written by this pass' both name it. team_constants above is transcribed by hand from that document's sec 8 table. Regenerate with `python tools/capacity_model.py --yaml` and diff before trusting it."
- "Every achievability weight is a DERIVATION from 04_predictive_factors.yaml, not a measurement. Four of the thirteen (A3, A7, A11, A13) have no PF analogue at all and are anchored to capacity-model constraint ranks instead. Their `from` fields say so explicitly."
- "The anchors are calibrated for inter-rater agreement, not for accuracy. Two people scoring the same design should land within 1 point; both can still be wrong about the design."
- "auto_score_from_corpus exists so the rubric has a 30-record regression test. It is NOT how you should score a real kickoff-day candidate -- score the factors by hand against the anchors, using the corpus only to check yourself."
- "A10 (cots_availability) has no source field in archetype_corpus.yaml and is proxied from mfg_floor. It is the weakest mapping here and it is worth 55/856 of the achievability index."
- "The quadrant thresholds (60 / 55) were set so the corpus's hand-labelled YES records fall right of the achievability line. That is calibration to 30 expert labels, not validation against outcomes."
- "No factor in this file, and no threshold, is derived from any BIOCORE rule. The game is not public. biocore_kickoff_inputs is the list of things that must be filled before the rubric produces a BIOCORE answer rather than a general one."
